/* Genesis 1:26 */
enhance("Genesis 1:26: “Let us make man in our image”",[
  N(cr('troki',IA_NOTES),
    "Elohim is a plural noun, so the Hebrew Bible already says God is plural in Genesis 1:1.",
    "Elohim is also used of angels, judges and Moses, and the plural form takes singular verbs when it means the one God. Plural of majesty, not plurality of persons.",
    "Elohim: a plural form, not a plural God",
    [
      "Elohim is used of angels (Manoah in Judges 13:22), human judges (Exod 22:9; 21:6) and Moses (“I have made thee an Elohim to Pharaoh”, Exod 7:1). No one would call Moses a visible Trinity. (Troki I.9; IA #seerahpro-bible-notes)",
      "Hebrew likewise uses Adonim for a single master (Isa 19:4; Gen 39:20; Exod 21:4), as modern languages use “you” for one person. (Troki I.9)",
      "If the plural form proved a Trinity, the singular Eloha (Deut 32:15; Ps 50:22) would disprove it, and Isaiah 44:6 and 44:8 use both forms of the same God. The alternation cannot carry doctrine. (Troki I.9)",
      "Divine names on non-divine beings: humans bear God's name (Jer 14:9; Isa 43:7), the angel carries it (Exod 23:21), and Jerusalem is called YHWH (Jer 33:16). (IA #seerahpro-bible-notes)"
    ],
    ["Genesis 1:1, 26; Exodus 7:1; 21:6; 22:9; Judges 13:22; Isaiah 19:4; 44:6, 8","Troki I.9; IA #seerahpro-bible-notes"],
    []),
  N(cr('troki','kravitz','cl'),
    "“Us” can mean persons within the one God, in the way that echad in the Shema means a compound one.",
    "Whatever “us” means, the Hebrew Bible says in many places that God is one and that no saviour exists beside him. A hint cannot override a statement.",
    "Even if “us” hinted at plurality, the unity texts are explicit",
    [
      "Strict unity texts: Deut 6:4, 4:35, 4:39; Isa 43:11, 44:6, 45:5-6, 40:18; Jer 10:6; Hos 13:4; Ps 86:10; Neh 9:6; 1 Chr 17:20 all assert a single God and no saviour beside him, so God cannot be divided into two or three. Troki adds that a divisible God would lack omnipresence, like created matter. (Troki I.10)",
      "“Let us” can be a plural of emphasis (Gen 11:7; Job 18:2) or God addressing his servants, since he makes his will known to them (Amos 3:7; Gen 18:17). The text then says “And God created man in His image” (singular), not “they created”. (Troki I.10)",
      "Echad: Kravitz answers the compound-one argument by saying that physical objects cannot describe God, and that Ecclesiastes 4:8 uses echad for “one, not two”. (Kravitz Claim #5, footnote)",
      "The guide notes that Robert M. Bowman, a Trinitarian, says all Trinitarians agree the doctrine is not found directly in the Old Testament (Why You Should Believe in the Trinity, p. 22). (Choose Life p. 56 fn. 9)",
      "Gen 18: the three men are angels in human form, sometimes called by God's name as His messengers (Kravitz fn. on Genesis 18). See the existing card on the three visitors."
    ],
    ["Deuteronomy 6:4; 4:35; Isaiah 44:6; 45:5-6; Genesis 11:7; 18:17; Amos 3:7; Ecclesiastes 4:8","Troki I.10; Kravitz Claim #5; Choose Life p. 56"],
    [])
]);

/* Son of Man, Daniel 7 */
enhance("The Son of Man (Daniel 7, 1 Enoch & Gospels): 4-dossier synthesis",[
  N(cr('troki'),
    "Daniel 7:13 foretells Jesus' second coming. Coming with the clouds shows a superhuman being whose dominion is everlasting.",
    "Daniel's own interpretation (7:18, 22, 27) gives the kingdom to “the saints of the Most High”, which is Israel. Clouds are a Biblical image of God's swift action.",
    "Daniel's own interpretation: the saints of the Most High",
    [
      "In 7:18, 22 and 27 the kingdom goes to the saints of the Most High. Israel is called the holy people (Ex 19:6; Deut 7:6; Isa 62:12; Dan 8:24; 12:7), and “Most High” is a title also given to Israel (Deut 26:19; 28:1). (Troki I.41)",
      "Clouds are used for God's decrees and swift action (Isa 19:1; Jer 4:13; Isa 60:8), and a dream does not require a supernatural fulfilment: the stone cut without hands (Dan 2:34) is a symbol. (Troki I.41)",
      "“All rulers shall serve him” did not happen under Jesus, since Jews, Muslims and others do not serve him. “All” is limited by Dan 7:27 and 2:44, where the kingdom under the whole heaven is given and the other kingdoms are destroyed (Isa 60:12). (Troki I.41)",
      "“Everlasting dominion” means hereditary and inalienable kingship, as for David's line, and does not need a divine being. (Troki I.41)"
    ],
    ["Daniel 7:13-14, 18, 22, 27; 2:34, 44; Isaiah 19:1; 60:12","Troki I.41"],
    []),
  N(cr(IA_NOTES),
    "In Mark 14:62 Jesus claims to be the Son of Man of Daniel 7, which is the claim to be divine.",
    "The IA notes argue the Mark 14 identification contradicts Daniel and is later scribal theology, and that “son of man” is an ordinary idiom for a human descendant.",
    "Mark 14:62, the idiom “son of man”, and where the kingdom goes",
    [
      "The notes argue that Jesus claiming to be the Son of Man in Mark 14 contradicts the book of Daniel and is an assertion by later scribes. “Son of man” is also the Aramaic and Arabic idiom for a human descendant (bani adam). This is their position, and it needs support from the textual evidence. (IA #seerahpro-bible-notes)",
      "The fifth kingdom of Daniel 2 is read as the kingdom of God of Daniel 7. Matthew 21:43 (the kingdom taken from them) and Matthew 25:34 (the kingdom prepared since the foundation of the world) are used to argue that the kingdom pre-exists and is handed on to others, rather than being identified with the person of Jesus. (IA #seerahpro-bible-notes)"
    ],
    ["Mark 14:62; Daniel 2, 7; Matthew 21:43; 25:34","IA #seerahpro-bible-notes"],
    [])
]);
